TI  - Use of ultrasound guidance to improve the safety of percutaneous dilatational 
      tracheostomy: a literature review.
PG  - 229
LID - 10.1186/s13054-015-0942-5 [doi]
LID - 229
AB  - Patients in ICUs frequently require tracheostomy for long-term ventilator 
      support, and the percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy (PDT) method is preferred 
      over surgical tracheostomy. The use of ultrasound (US) imaging to guide ICU 
      procedures and interventions has recently emerged as a simple and noninvasive 
      tool. The current evidence regarding the applications of US in PDT is 
      encouraging; however, the exact role of US-guided dilatational tracheostomy 
      (US-PDT) remains poorly defined. In this review, we describe the best available 
      evidence concerning the safety and efficacy of US-PDT and briefly delineate the 
      general principles of US image generation for the airway and of US-PDT 
      procedures.
