IS  - 0001-5172 (Print)
IS  - 0001-5172 (Linking)
VI  - 51
IP  - 3
DP  - 2007 Mar
TI  - Repeat percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy in an awake and unintubated 
      patient.
PG  - 378-9
AB  - Bedside percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy (PDT) has become an accepted 
      method for securing airways in patients requiring prolonged ventilatory support. 
      Repeat PDT in patients who have had a tracheostomy earlier is considered a 
      relative contraindication as a result of distorted anatomy. Three case series 
      have been reported supporting the safety of repeat bedside PDT in experienced 
      hands, but there is no previously published data regarding repeat PDT in awake 
      and unintubated patients. We report a case in which a repeat PDT was done on an 
      awake 20-year-old female patient with cerebral palsy and kyphoscoliosis admitted 
      owing to pneumonia and respiratory failure. This facilitated tracheal toilet in 
      this patient without resorting to general anaesthesia and mechanical ventilation. 
      In conclusion, both repeat PDT and awake PDT, both separately or together, can be 
      very useful in the management of critical care and pre-critical care patients and 
      merit wider use.
