DP  - 2016 Mar
TI  - Inadvertent migration of guidewire into Murphy's eye of endotracheal tube during 
      percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy.
PG  - 188-90
LID - 10.4103/0972-5229.178186 [doi]
AB  - Percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy is a commonly performed bedside procedure 
      in the Intensive Care Unit. Although serious and fatal complications have been 
      reported, the procedure is by and large safe to perform in experienced hands. We 
      report here an innocuous problem encountered twice. After the guidewire insertion 
      and dilatation, subsequent railroading became difficult owing to migration of 
      guidewire into the Murphy's eye of the endotracheal tube (ETT). Awareness about 
      this possibility can avert inadvertent delays and complications during the 
      procedure. A tug or gentle pulling of ETT after insertion of the guidewire rules 
      out an impaction in the eye or other part of the ETT.
